post_install() {
    cat <<'EOF'

Polyseat is installed. Two things are left, and a package is not allowed to do
either of them for you.

  1. Get the machine ready:

       sudo polyseat-prepare

     It writes the idmap range every container start needs, brings up Incus and
     initialises it if nobody has, checks that the graphics driver answers, and
     puts your account in the input group. It says what it finds either way and
     changes nothing that is already right.

  2. Start it and claim it:

       sudo systemctl enable --now polyseatd

     Then open https://<this machine>:47800. Nobody has claimed it yet, so the
     page asks you to choose a password rather than to type one. Do that before
     anybody else on the network does.

Two things worth knowing before you build a seat:

  * The shared game library needs a filesystem that can share blocks, which
    means btrfs or XFS made with reflink=1. Seats work without one; the library
    simply stays off, and polyseat-prepare says which you have.

  * On an AMD card, you are the first. That path was written and reasoned about
    without one to run it on. It is expected to work and it may well not, and
    either answer is worth an issue with the output of: sudo polyseatd -report

EOF
}

post_remove() {
    if systemctl is-active --quiet polyseatd.service; then
        cat <<'EOF'

polyseatd is still running, from a binary that is no longer on disk. Removing a
package does not stop a service, and this one is deliberately not stopped for
you.

  sudo systemctl stop polyseatd

Your seats keep running either way. They are Incus containers and Incus owns
them; the daemon supervises them and does not hold them up.

EOF
    fi

    cat <<'EOF'

Two things are deliberately left behind, because they are yours and not the
package's:

  /var/lib/polyseat   the seats, their pairings and the interface password
  /srv/polyseat       the shared game library

Your seats themselves are still there as Incus containers. `incus list` shows
them. Install Polyseat again and it picks them up where they were.

EOF
}

post_upgrade() {
    cat <<'EOF'

Polyseat is upgraded on disk. The process that is running is still the old one:
replacing a binary does not disturb a process using it.

  sudo systemctl restart polyseatd

Seats keep running through that. Their containers are not stopped and are picked
up again on the way back in, so nobody's game ends. What does happen is that
each seat's input broker restarts with the daemon, so a controller can drop for
a moment.

If this version builds seats differently, the web interface names the seats that
are behind and offers one button to bring them up to date, at a moment you pick.

EOF
}
